        "note": "100% of the claim's wording appears in the reference article, including 1 number(s)",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "wikipedia-lexical"
      },
      {
        "claim": "John Martyn Harlow took charge of Gage's treatment, including draining an abscess, and published the account in which friends said he was no longer Gage only in 1868, after Gage's death.",
        "segment_index": 3,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "Wikipedia: Harlow took charge around 6 pm, drained a later abscess, and published the no-longer-Gage account in 1868, after Gage's death; Simply Psychology notes Harlow's original account was later embellished.",
        "correction": null,
        "method": "claude-research",
        "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phineas_Gage"
      },
      {
        "claim": "Gage worked as a long-distance stagecoach driver on the Valparaiso to Santiago route in Chile from 1852, and CT studies of his actual skull concluded only the left frontal lobe was damaged.",
        "segment_index": 4,
        "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
        "note": "Wikipedia gives the 1852 move to Chile and the demanding Valparaiso to Santiago stagecoach work, and the Ratiu and Van Horn CT studies of the actual skull concluding the left frontal lobe was damaged and the right was not, correcting the 1994 Damasio study. Simply Psychology and Macmillan agree the damage was less extensive than popularly assumed.",
        "correction": null,
